WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:03.035
SPEAKER: When I took
the first call about this case,

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:06.605
which was indicating that
two people may have been shot,

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:07.807
many years ago,

00:00:08.307 --> 00:00:10.110
and they had been buried
in their own back garden,

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.079
part of my reaction was,

00:00:14.213 --> 00:00:16.116
surely that can't have happened,
can it?

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:18.017
♪ (MELLOW MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:20.753
CAROLINE CRAMPTON:
You know those stories
that you puzzle over,

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.089
that you just can't
get out of your head?

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.525
Well, this is one of those.

00:00:27.693 --> 00:00:31.498
<i> Landscapers</i> is a new four-part
drama from HBO and Sky.

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:35.468
It follows the true story of
Susan and Christopher Edwards,

00:00:36.202 --> 00:00:38.671
a soft-spoken couple
with a gruesome secret.

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:43.276
I'm Caroline Crampton,
a longtime fan of crime fiction

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:45.444
and the true stories
that inspire it.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:50.116
In this five-episode companion
podcast from HBO and Sky,

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.352
I'm gonna take you
behind the scenes
of<i> Landscapers.</i>

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.356
Together, we'll unpack
how a team of filmmakers

00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:58.524
adapted the chilling
true crime story

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:02.195
for the limited series
starring Olivia Colman
and David Thewlis.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.264
♪ (DRAMATIC MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:10.369
ED SINCLAIR:
Rightly, there's a lot of energy
devoted to the victims,

00:01:11.003 --> 00:01:13.906
but I think there should be
a lot of energy also devoted

00:01:14.006 --> 00:01:17.310
to the thinking
of the perpetrators.

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:20.980
CRAMPTON: That's Ed Sinclair,
the series creator.

00:01:21.781 --> 00:01:25.451
While writing<i> Landscapers,</i>
he examined every part
of the case,

00:01:25.951 --> 00:01:27.286
from a backyard burial

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:29.355
to an obsession
with film memorabilia,

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:32.792
and he worked with the team
to bring the story to life.

00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:35.662
SPEAKER 2: It kind of starts out
as like a one-line pitch,

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:39.031
but the more you put in it,
the more it kind of unravels

00:01:39.131 --> 00:01:42.034
into this kind of sprawling,
multifaceted,

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:44.770
incredibly complicated
exploration.

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:48.340
SPEAKER 3: As the actor, there's
always got to be a line you draw

00:01:48.907 --> 00:01:53.212
because it isn't a documentary,
and it isn't a recreation.

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:54.814
It's a kind of exploration.

00:01:56.148 --> 00:01:59.385
CRAMPTON: For some of the team,
adapting this story wasn't easy.

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:03.856
As they learned about the case,
it seeped in and took over.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:05.424
SPEAKER 4: I had nightmares
about it

00:02:05.524 --> 00:02:07.560
when I first
started researching.

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:10.997
Not because of the horror,

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:14.133
so much of the violence
of what happened,

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:16.335
but just trying to imagine
what it's like

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:18.771
to live with that kind of guilt.

00:02:20.038 --> 00:02:22.709
CRAMPTON: We also talked to
the real people behind the case,

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.310
the one's who experienced it
first-hand.

00:02:25.677 --> 00:02:28.547
For them,
this story isn't a TV drama.

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:29.915
It's their lives.

00:02:30.015 --> 00:02:31.250
♪ (MELANCHOLY MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:32.752
SPEAKER 5: I remember
it was a rainy night.

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:33.986
I actually walked

00:02:34.386 --> 00:02:36.289
to the police station
from my house.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:38.958
SPEAKER 6: It was
an absolute media circus,

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:40.493
absolutely packed
to the rafters.

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:43.796
SPEAKER 7:
There's probably something
slightly salacious in it.

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:47.099
SPEAKER 8: I-- I like them.
I don't know how far

00:02:47.199 --> 00:02:49.001
I can elaborate on that,
but I do.

00:02:49.468 --> 00:02:50.636
SPEAKER: This one
just fascinates me.

00:02:50.736 --> 00:02:53.539
It almost became an obsession,
I think.

00:02:54.506 --> 00:02:56.542
SPEAKER 9: I- I can't say
I feel sorry for them,

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:57.544
because I don't.

00:02:58.477 --> 00:03:01.213
Let's not forget
what they've done here

00:03:01.313 --> 00:03:04.050
is murdered
two completely innocent people

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.586
and taken them for every single
penny that they've got.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:12.858
CRAMPTON:<i> Landscapers</i> airs
from December 6th on HBO

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:14.927
and from December 7th on Sky.

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:17.897
The<i> Landscapers</i> podcast
arrives December 6th,

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:19.932
with episodes released weekly.

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:22.535
Get ready to find out
what's really buried

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:24.404
in the ground beneath your feet.

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:28.608
♪ (OPERA MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:31.644
SPEAKER 10:
Murder is a tragic event.

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:35.415
In the end, that felt like
the sort of truest thing.

00:03:36.649 --> 00:03:38.384
That we can never actually know

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:41.421
the inner workings
of another human being.

